#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "b10aaa3e-2ba4-51af-be3b-056d72692885" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-10889" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given above. Find $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1117/Source/Images/abe66489d05fd250027ac965d13113c8.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)\n$$\n\nwe need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in [1,2]$. Notice that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nlies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1117/Source/Images/bddea9186b1d9ddf86f3bf2672bbcd36.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx} &= \\frac{0 - 2}{2 - 1} = \\frac{-2}{1} =-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)=-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096299 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9811f16e-75aa-5982-b97b-cc59b33fb7d8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-10890" :question/problem "The graphs of the functions $y = f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(-1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1117/Source/Images/36fb139722771111b1f567de83a5ef7e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x) + g'(x)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(-1) =f'(-1) + g'(-1)\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,1)$ for the function $f$, and\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,0)$ for the function $g$,\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1117/Source/Images/9a5f87b5ba64614056ff71db4da844c7.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,1)$ and equals\n$\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{1-(-1)}{1-(-2)} = \\dfrac{2}{3}$.\nTherefore, $f'(-1)=\\dfrac{2}{3}$.\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,0)$ and equals\n$\\dfrac{\\Delta g}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{-1-2}{0-(-2)} = -\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\nTherefore, $g'(-1)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(-1) &= f^{′}(-1) + g^{′}(-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096300 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "1fb2491b-f442-57e3-990f-65bf1270d847" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-10891" :question/problem "The graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(0)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1117/Source/Images/2b67c1252910191313060f3adfeb1904.png)" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot g'(x)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(0)=f'(0)\\cdot g(0)+f(0)\\cdot g'(0)\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(0)=1\n$$\n\nand $g(0)=-1$.\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(-1,1)$ equals $-1$. So, $f'(0)=-1$.\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(-0.5,1)$ equals $2$. So, $g'(0)=2$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(0) &= f^{′}(0) \\cdot g(0) + f(0) \\cdot g^{′}(0) \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096301 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5e929c25-68b1-5a6b-92a0-d12b90a112e8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-10892" :question/problem "Given the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find $h'(2)$ where $h(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1117/Source/Images/f542de811e465d54bf83b21ffde6a218.png)" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = \\dfrac{f'(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot g'(x)}{(g(x))^2}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(2) = \\dfrac{f'(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot g'(2)}{(g(2))^2}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(2)=1\n$$\n\nand $g(2)=0.5$.\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-1$. So, $f'(2)=-1$.\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-0.5$. So, $g'(2)=-0.5$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(2) &= (f^{′}(2) \\cdot g(2) - f(2) \\cdot g^{′}(2))/((g(2))^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((-1) \\cdot (0.5) - (1) \\cdot (-0.5))/((0.5)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab82f947-c9cf-506b-b6fb-f70a9ddfbb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095444} {:answer/id #uuid "f36b03d0-0103-5419-a888-06d4571c1d1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095445} {:answer/id #uuid "338ae95f-7435-5b74-89a7-674db385a109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095446} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1b9408-a98f-5cc9-9bec-055abdd921a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095447} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7b129a-8c50-54c9-ae2a-4a4cce1f3ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c1b9408-a98f-5cc9-9bec-055abdd921a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095447} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8c4d24-4c73-5f34-981f-066aaed1e55b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095443}] :question/id #uuid "4a073ec9-0e41-5290-8f8f-28a860565103" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48944" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1117/Source/Images/q-48944.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x)$ above, find $h^{′}(\\frac{7}{2})$ where $h(x) = (f(x))/(g(x))$."}]}
